scard T5, I keep T6 pair and T8 pair, which might form sequences with T4, T7, etc.  \n\nI think discarding W9 is reasonable because it's a terminal and less connected to my hand. Also, from unshown tiles, W9 has 3 left, so not too rare.  \n\nBut let's see valid actions: Play T6; Play W9; Play T5; Play J2; Play W8; Play T8; Play W7.  \n\nI should avoid discarding J2 because it's a dragon pair that can be a pung for points. Avoid discarding W8 or T8 because they are pairs that can be part of sequences or become pungs. Discarding T6 breaks a pair but T6 is a middle tile, might be dangerous.  \n\nSo among W9, T5, W7 \u2014 W7 is needed for sequence, so not W7. Between W9 and T5, I think T5 might be more useful because it can combine with T6 to form sequences. W9 is terminal and only connects with W7-W8 if I have W9, but I already have W9, so actually W9 is part of the W7-W8-W9 sequence. Wait, I have W7, W8, W8, W9 \u2014 so W9 is needed to complete the Chow with W7 and one W8. If I discard W9, I no longer have that sequence. So discarding W9 might be bad because it breaks a potential meld.  \n\nActually, I have two W8s, so I can use one W8 with W7 and W9 to form a Chow, and the other W8 remains. So W9 is important. Then maybe discard T5 instead.  \n\nT5 is a single, not part of any sequence yet (though with T6 it could be). But I have two T6s, so T5 and T6 could form a Chow with T4 or T7. So T5 has potential.  \n\nBut I need to think about quickest route to winning. I have one meld, one potential meld (W7-W8-W9), and two pairs (J2 and T8). I need two more melds. The T5 and T6 could become part of a meld if I draw well.  \n\nMaybe discard W8? But W8 is a pair and part of sequence.  \n\nGiven the game state, I think discarding T5 is a reasonable safe discard that doesn't break important pairs or sequences.  \n\nReason: T5 is a middle tile but not heavily discarded; it maintains my pairs and potential sequences with T6. W9 is terminal but part of a near-complete Chow, so keeping it is better.  \n\nThus, I choose Play T5.  \n\nAnswer: Play T5"}
