scarded, so might be risky. T8 has been discarded (by across: Play T8), so T8 might be safer. W9 discarded by me, so W9 is safe. W7 not discarded, might be risky. W8 discarded by me, so safe.\n\nBut also consider opponents' possible needs: Next player has T9 pung and B1B2B3 chow. They might be going for All Types or something. Across has T7T8T9 chow. Previous has two low chows. None seem to be close to high-value hands, but could be waiting.\n\nMy hand potential: If I keep W7,W8,W9, I have a chow. If I discard J3, I keep two pairs and a chow. Then I need either T6 pung or T8 pung or a chow involving T6 or T8. T6 pair: need one more T6 (2 left). T8 pair: need one more T8 (2 left? unshown T8:2). Also could get T7 to form T6T7T8? That would use one T6 and one T8, leaving one T6 and one T8 as pair? Actually if I get T7, I could form chow T6T7T8 using one T6 and one T8, then I have leftover T6 and T8 as a pair? But then I would have: chow W7W8W9, chow T6T7T8, pair T6? No, after using one T6 and one T8 for chow, I have one T6 and one T8 left, which are not a pair. So I would need another pair. Alternatively, if I get T7, I could form T6T7T8 and then have pair J3 if I keep J3. So maybe keeping J3 is useful as potential pair.\n\nIf I discard T6 or T8, I break a pair, which reduces my pair count. I currently have two pairs, which is good because I need a pair to win. If I break one, I only have one pair, and then I need to form another pair from somewhere, which is harder. So better to keep pairs.\n\nIf I discard W7, W8, or W9, I break the potential chow. That might be okay if I can form other melds. But the W7W8W9 chow is ready if I keep them. So discarding one of them would lose that meld.\n\nGiven I have two pairs, it's often good to keep pairs because they can become pungs or remain as pair. So discard a single tile that is not part of a pair or potential meld. My singles: W7, W8, W9, J3. But W7,W8,W9 together form a potential chow, so they are not entirely singles; they are connected. J3 is a single honor. Which is more useless? J3 can pair with drawn J3 to make a pair, or get two more for pung. But as an honor, it's harder to get because there are only 3 left, and one has been discarded. But if I get another J3, I have a pair, which could be my winning pair. Or if I get two more, I have a pung. But J3 pung would give points (Dragon Pung, 2 points). Also, my other meld is J2 pung, so if I get J3 pung, I could have Two Dragon Pungs (6 points). That's valuable. So keeping J3 might be good for potential Two Dragon Pungs.\n\nBut to win, I need a complete hand. With J3 single, I need to either make it a pair (by drawing another) or use it in a chow? No, honors can't be in chow. So J3 must be part of a pung or pair. So if I keep J3, I need to get another J3 for pair, or two more for pung. That's possible (3 left). If I discard J3, then I have no honors in hand except the J2 meld, and I have two pairs (T6 and T8). Then I need to form two more melds from W7,W8,W9 and the pairs. W7W8W9 is one meld. Then I have T6 pair and T8 pair. I need to convert one pair into a meld and keep the other as pair. For T6 to become a meld, I need T4,T5 or T5,T7 or T7,T8? Actually T6 can form chow with T4T5T6 or T5T6T7. That would use one T6 and two other tiles. If I use T6 in a chow, I break the T6 pair, leaving one T6. Then I would have T8 pair as my pair. So I need to draw T4 and T5, or T5 and T7, etc. That's two specific draws. Similarly for T8, to form a chow, I need T6T7T8 or T7T8T9, but T9 is dead (0 left), so only T6T7T8 is possible, which would use one T8 and T6,T7. But I have T6 pair, so I could use one T6 and one T8 and draw T7 to form T6T7T8 chow. That would use one T6 and one T8, leaving one T6 and one T8, which are not a pair. So then I would need to make a pair from something else, like W7 or W9 etc. That's messy.\n\nAlternatively, if I keep J3 and discard one of the W tiles, say W9, then I have: W7,W8 (need W9 or W6 for chow), T6 pair, T8 pair, J3 single. Then I need to complete W7W8W9 chow by drawing W9 (3 left) or form W6W7W8 by drawing W6 (4 left). And I need to make a meld from T6/T8 or get J3 pair/pung. So still need two melds.\n\nMaybe the best discard is W9 because it's a terminal and has been discarded by me already, so safe. Also, keeping W7 and W8 gives potential chow with W6 or W9. But W9 is terminal, and terminals are often dangerous, but since it's already discarded, it's safe.\n\nDiscard T6 or T8 would break a pair, which is generally bad when you have few pairs.\n\nDiscard J3 might be okay if I want to go for a hand without more honors, but then I lose Two Dragon Pungs potential.\n\nGiven the scoring patterns, my current hand has J2 pung (Dragon Pung, 2 points). If I get J3 pung, that's another Dragon Pung, total 4 points from two dragon pungs, plus possibly Two Dragon Pungs pattern (6 points) so total 8 points? Actually Two Dragon Pungs is 6 points, and each Dragon Pung is 2 points each, but patterns are not additive that way? In MCR, you sum the points of all patterns that apply. So if I have two dragon pungs, I get Dragon Pung (2 points) for each, so 4 points, plus Two Dragon Pungs (6 points) = 10 points. That's enough to win (>=8). So that's a good target. So keeping J3 is important for that.\n\nAlso, I have a chow of B2B3B4, which is all simples (no terminals/honors) so might give All Simples (2 points) if my whole hand has no terminals/honors. But J2 pung and J3 would be honors, so not All Simples. So if I go for Two Dragon Pungs, I will have honors, so not All Simples.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard J3, I could aim for All Simples (2 points) plus maybe other patterns. But I already have J2 pung, which is an honor, so I cannot have All Simples because J2 is an honor. So All Simples is impossible. So I must have honors in my hand due to J2 meld. So I might as well embrace honors and go for Two Dragon Pungs.\n\nTherefore, I should keep J3 and try to get another J3. So discard a tile that doesn't help that plan.\n\nWhich tile to discard? Among W7,W8,W9,T6,T8. I need to keep pairs T6 and T8 for potential pungs or chows. I also have W7,W8,W9 as potential chow. But if I discard one of them, I break the chow possibility. But maybe I don't need that chow if I can form other melds. However, having a ready chow is good because it's one meld already.\n\nIf I keep W7,W8,W9, that's a chow, so I have 3 melds (B chow, J2 pung, W chow). Then I need one more meld and a pair. My hand would be: T6 pair, T8 pair, J3 single. So I need to either make a meld from T6/T8 or from J3. J3 needs two more, so that's a meld (pung). Or T6 needs one more for pung, or T8 needs one more for pung. Or I could make a chow using T6 and T8 with T7. So actually, with T6 pair and T8 pair, I have two pairs, which means I have a pair already (I can use one pair as the winning pair). But I need one more meld. So if I keep both pairs, I can use one pair as the final pair, and the other pair needs to become a meld (by getting a third tile). So I need to draw either a third T6 or a third T8 or a T7 to form a chow with one T6 and one T8. So there are several outs: T6 (2 left), T8 (2 left), T7 (3 left). That's 7 outs total. Plus if I go for J3 pung, I need two J3 (3 left), but that's harder because need two draws. So better to aim for T6/T8 pung or T7 chow.\n\nIf I discard J3, then I have: chow W7W8W9, chow B2B3B4, pung J2, and hand: T6 pair, T8 pair. That's 4 melds? Wait: I have 3 melds already (B chow, J2 pung, W chow) and hand has T6 pair and T8 pair. That's only 13 tiles? Let's count: melds: 3+3+3=9 tiles, hand: T6,T6,T8,T8 =4 tiles, total 13. I need 14 tiles to win. So I am missing one tile. Actually, a complete hand is 14 tiles: 4 melds (3 tiles each) + 1 pair (2 tiles) = 14. Currently I have 3 melds (9 tiles) and 2 pairs (4 tiles) = 13 tiles. So I have one extra tile? That can't be. I must have miscounted. My hand tiles count: according to state, hand tiles are [W7:1; W8:1; W9:1; T6:2; 